#
# ## 5. Interpretation
#
# **What the results show, against the three things the challenge asks a comparison to explain:**{{ chk() }}
#
# - **Fidelity against the operations compilation added.** Under one noise model, F tracks the two-qubit gates and the idle time the circuit adds. The baseline's SWAPs out and back cost {{ c[2].two_qubit_gates }}, {{ c[8].two_qubit_gates }} and {{ c[5].two_qubit_gates }} two-qubit gates; the move chain's {{ mv.ibm2016.two_qubit_gates }}, {{ mv.hyperbolic.two_qubit_gates }} and {{ mv.contemporary.two_qubit_gates }} recover most of the lost fidelity on every graph.
# - **Whether protection pays.** A check pays when it reads at the end of the circuit, on qubits the pair never used: on the contemporary and hyperbolic-inspired graphs it raises F by more than twice its standard error while discarding {{ pct(1 - c[9].acceptance) }} to {{ pct(1 - c[6].acceptance) }} of shots. A check that must read mid-circuit, on the 2016-inspired graph, holds the pair idle while it reads and does not raise F beyond its standard error at any measurement error we set.
# - **Connectivity against noise.** Every graph runs under the same profile, so the differences are connectivity's: hops between the ports set the unprotected cost, and disjoint paths decide whether a check can avoid a mid-circuit read.

# %% [markdown]
# <a id="s6"></a>
#
# ## 6. Limitations
#
# **These results describe circuits under one assumed noise model, simulated; they are not a measurement of any device.**{{ chk() }}
#
# - **One noise model:** Boston's calibration medians. Its readout pair puts every absolute F about 0.002 high, equally on all three graphs, so comparisons between graphs stand. What it leaves out, and why, is in [DESIGN.md, section 4]({{ link("docs/DESIGN.md") }}#4-noise-model).
# - **How far the results hold:** four optional studies each vary one assumption under the same profile ([appendix A](#sA)). case 3's check does not beat the move chain alone even with no mid-circuit measurement error; the move chain's lead over the baseline on the contemporary graph is gone by a starting excitation of 0.005; moving the pair out from the middle gives no gain we claim; and a check that reads the emptied path at the end beats case 3 on every measure.
# - **One seed per run:** a difference smaller than about 0.001 needs more seeds before it means anything.
# - **Detection, not correction:** a check discards shots and corrects nothing, and no fault injector has tested the checks fault by fault.
# - **Idle qubits start in |0⟩:** the move chain and the spare-path checks rely on it; appendix A.2 shows how fast the lead shrinks when they do not.
# - **No degree penalty:** case 6's check passes through the contemporary graph's five-edge qubit with no extra error.
# - **The graphs:** the contemporary graph is the organisers' 9-node graph, not a real IBM layout, and the hyperbolic-inspired graph is a schematic.
